    WS-Addressing.

    @section xmlsoapversions SOAP Versions

    SOAP 1.1 and SOAP 1.2 are supported. There is no SOAP 1.0 standard to support: the 1999 drafts sometimes called
    SOAP 1.0 (for example the IETF Internet-Draft
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-box-http-soap-01">draft-box-http-soap-01</a>) were
    pre-standard proposals that were superseded by the SOAP 1.1 W3C Note of May 2000, the first published
    specification and the oldest version that SOAP implementations interoperate with.

    The SOAP version of a message is selected by the WSDL binding of the operation: a service with both SOAP 1.1 and
    SOAP 1.2 bindings can be called and served in either version, and each binding uses its own envelope namespace,
    media type, action handling, fault format and encoding rules. @ref SoapHandler::SoapHandler "SoapHandler" answers an envelope of a SOAP
    version that the service does not support with a \c VersionMismatch fault carrying an \c Upgrade header that
    names the supported envelope.

    @section xmlsoapdecisions Behavior Choices

    Where the standards leave a choice, the modules behave as follows:
    - XML Schema \c Name, \c NCName, \c QName and \c NMTOKEN values, and the types derived from them, use the name
      characters of XML 1.0 Second Edition for every SOAP version, as XML Schema 1.0 requires
    - an omitted accessor of an RPC message is decoded as an absent value (@ref nothing "NOTHING") for literal and
      encoded messages; an empty accessor is an empty value

    @section xmlsoaplimits Limitations

    - only the HTTP transport is implemented; WSDL documents with other bindings (for example Apache CXF's XML and JMS
      bindings) can be loaded, and their bindings are reported as unsupported
    - WS-* specifications other than WS-Addressing, such as WS-Security, WS-ReliableMessaging and WS-Policy
      assertions other than WS-Addressing's, are not implemented
    - the WS-I requirements that concern UDDI registries and HTTP cookies do not apply to these modules

    @section xmlsoapexamples Examples

    See @ref xmlcookbooksoap "the cookbook" for a complete client and server example, and the documentation of each
    module for its options.
